WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.947 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Could a large multiplication become three familiar facts?

00:00:04.597 --> 00:00:09.648 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are multiplying efficiently by breaking numbers
into place-value parts.

00:00:09.868 --> 00:00:15.635 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will multiply each part and recombine the results, while
keeping track of what each digit is worth.

00:00:16.285 --> 00:00:21.510 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to multiply whole numbers efficiently
by partitioning place values.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.911 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:24.131 --> 00:00:27.368 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can split a number into useful place-value parts.

00:00:27.588 --> 00:00:30.939 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can multiply every part and combine the products.

00:00:31.159 --> 00:00:33.816 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use an estimate to check the answer.

00:00:34.466 --> 00:00:43.226 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Partitioning uses the distributive property: multiplying a
sum gives the same result as multiplying each part and
adding the partial products.

00:00:43.446 --> 00:00:48.411 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Hundreds stay hundreds, tens stay tens, and ones stay ones
until we regroup.

00:00:48.631 --> 00:00:52.021 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture three equal rows with four counters in each.

00:00:52.241 --> 00:00:56.538 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Splitting each row into two and two does not change the
twelve counters.

00:00:57.188 --> 00:01:00.300 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Calculate three hundred and twenty-four times eight.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:04.744 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Split three hundred and twenty-four into three hundred,
twenty and four.

00:01:05.394 --> 00:01:10.436 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twenty times eight is one hundred and sixty, and four times
eight is thirty-two.

00:01:10.656 --> 00:01:15.624 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Combine all three partial products to get two thousand five
hundred and ninety-two.

00:01:16.274 --> 00:01:19.092 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try four hundred and twenty-six times seven.

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:22.503 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four hundred times seven gives two thousand eight hundred.

00:01:23.153 --> 00:01:27.901 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twenty times seven is one hundred and forty; six times seven
is forty-two.

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:31.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Their sum is two thousand nine hundred and eighty-two.

00:01:31.869 --> 00:01:35.190 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A result near three thousand fits our estimate.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.415 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Remember to multiply every part, including the ones.

00:01:39.635 --> 00:01:44.180 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eight groups of four ones make thirty-two, not just four
left unchanged.

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.692 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Combine all the partial products so none of the original
amount is lost.

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:52.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is two hundred and three times four?

00:01:56.018 --> 00:02:00.192 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two hundred times four is eight hundred, and three times
four is twelve.

00:02:00.412 --> 00:02:03.131 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add them to get eight hundred and twelve.

00:02:03.781 --> 00:02:08.495 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Partition by place value, multiply every part, then add the
partial products.

00:02:08.715 --> 00:02:13.725 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the value of each digit clear and use an estimate to
check the scale of your answer.

00:02:13.945 --> 00:02:17.248 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a model and explain why your strategy works.

00:02:17.468 --> 00:02:20.580 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the lesson practice on MathMagic!
